This sample program illustrates how the parameters of an optimization problem are
authored with Geneva's flat genome. Parameters are declared once through the
GGenomeBuilder fluent API (addDouble / addFloat / addInt32 / addBool, in single,
shared-group and per-parameter-array shapes, plain or constrained, each optionally
carrying a Gauss / bi-Gauss / integer-Gauss / flip adaptor), rather than by pushing
back individual parameter objects.

The program builds several example genomes and prints their shape, dumps a complete
GFunctionIndividual genome to a JSON document (result.json), and demonstrates inspecting
and mutating a minimal flat individual (streamline / boundaries / adapt / toCSV).
